The Pauli-matrix identity
\[
\overline{\sigma}^{\mu}\sigma^{\nu}
+\overline{\sigma}^{\nu}\sigma^{\mu}
=2\eta^{\mu\nu}I_2
\]
fixes the metric-dependent terms in the trace. Thus
\[
\operatorname{tr}\!\left(
\overline{\sigma}^{\mu}\sigma^{\nu}
\overline{\sigma}^{\rho}\sigma^{\sigma}\right)
=2\left(
\eta^{\mu\nu}\eta^{\rho\sigma}
-\eta^{\mu\rho}\eta^{\nu\sigma}
+\eta^{\mu\sigma}\eta^{\nu\rho}
\right)+C\epsilon^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}.
\]

To fix the sign, take \((\mu,\nu,\rho,\sigma)=(0,1,2,3)\):
\[
\operatorname{tr}\!\left(I_2\sigma^1(-\sigma^2)\sigma^3\right)
=-2i.
\]
The given matrices imply \(\eta=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)\), so \(\epsilon_{0123}=1\) means \(\epsilon^{0123}=-1\). Hence \(C=2i\), giving
\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{tr}\!\left(
\overline{\sigma}^{\mu}\sigma^{\nu}
\overline{\sigma}^{\rho}\sigma^{\sigma}
\right)
=
2\left(
\eta^{\mu\nu}\eta^{\rho\sigma}
-\eta^{\mu\rho}\eta^{\nu\sigma}
+\eta^{\mu\sigma}\eta^{\nu\rho}
+i\epsilon^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}
\right)
}.
\]

The momentum conjugate to \(\phi\) is
\[
\Pi(x)=\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial(\partial_0\phi)}
=\partial^0\phi^*(x)=\partial_0\phi^*(x),
\]
where the last equality uses the metric \((+,-,-,-)\).

Taking the Hermitian conjugate of the field expansion gives
\[
\hat\phi^\dagger(x)=
\int\frac{\mathrm d^3\vec p}{(2\pi)^3\,2\omega_{\vec p}}
\left[\hat a^\dagger(\vec p)e^{ip\cdot x}
+\hat b(\vec p)e^{-ip\cdot x}\right].
\]
Since \(p^0=\omega_{\vec p}=\sqrt{\vec p^{\,2}+m^2}\), differentiating with respect to time yields
\[
\boxed{
\hat\Pi(x)=
i\int\frac{\mathrm d^3\vec p}{(2\pi)^3\,2\omega_{\vec p}}\,
\omega_{\vec p}
\left[
\hat a^\dagger(\vec p)e^{ip\cdot x}
-\hat b(\vec p)e^{-ip\cdot x}
\right].
}
\]

