reference_solution:
The Noether charge can be written as $\mathcal{Q}=\int d^3x\,\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial(\partial_0A_\mu^a)}\delta A_\mu^a$. The gauge-field Lagrangian is
$$
\mathcal{L}
=
-\frac{1}{4}F_{a}^{\mu\nu}F^{a}_{\mu\nu}
-\frac{\theta e^{2}}{32\pi^{2}}
F_{a}^{\mu\nu}\widetilde{F}^{a}_{\mu\nu}
+\cdots .
$$
Then, we have
$$
\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}
{\partial(\partial_0A_\mu^a)}
=
-\frac{1}{2}F_b^{\alpha\beta}
\frac{\partial F_{\alpha\beta}^b}
{\partial(\partial_0A_\mu^a)}
-
\frac{\theta e^2}{32\pi^2}
\epsilon^{\rho\sigma\alpha\beta}
(F_b)_{\alpha\beta}
\frac{\partial F_{\rho\sigma}^b}
{\partial(\partial_0A_\mu^a)}.
$$
From the definition of $F_{\rho\sigma}^b$,
$$
\frac{\partial F_{\rho\sigma}^b}
{\partial(\partial_0A_\mu^a)}
=
\delta_a^b
\left(
\delta_\rho^0\delta_\sigma^\mu
-
\delta_\sigma^0\delta_\rho^\mu
\right).
$$
Substituting this expression and simplifying, we obtain
$$
\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}
{\partial(\partial_0A_i^a)}
=
-F_a^{0i}
-
\frac{\theta e^2}{16\pi^2}
\epsilon^{0ijk}(F_a)_{jk}.
$$
Using $F_a^{0i}=-(E_a)_i$ and $\epsilon^{0ijk}(F_a)_{jk}=-2(B_a)_i$, it follows that
$$
\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}
{\partial(\partial_0A_i^a)}
=
(E_a)_i
+
\frac{\theta e^2}{8\pi^2}(B_a)_i.
$$
Therefore, the Noether charge associated with the transformation $\delta A_\mu^a=\frac{1}{ev}D_\mu\phi^a$ is
$$
\mathcal{Q}
=
\int d^3x\,\frac{1}{ev}
\left[
(E_i)^a
+
\frac{\theta e^2}{8\pi^2}(B_i)^a
\right]D_i\phi^a .
$$
Using
$$
Q=\frac{1}{v}\int d^3x\,(E_i)^aD_i\phi^a,
\qquad
g=\frac{1}{v}\int d^3x\,(B_i)^aD_i\phi^a,
$$
we obtain
$$
\mathcal{Q}
=
\frac{Q}{e}
+
\frac{\theta e g}{8\pi^2}.
$$

